    {
      "name": "a rotation carrying no commitment is refused",
      "law": "The envelope",
      "obligations": ["XI-5", "VII-3", "IV-2"],
      "derivation": "**The last of four exchanges in which the being under this door has been left entirely alone, and that is Article IV's obligation rather than Article XI's.** \"**A being's digest never changes for the life of its pk.** A different class is a different being, minted beside the old.\" Every exchange in this scenario reads the whole record back, digest included, and across a run in which **the standing changed hands, an ask was answered, two were met with silence and a name was asked after**, the digest is one value throughout. That is the whole of what a lifetime invariant can be held to from outside: no single message could witness it, because it is a claim about what did *not* happen over a door's run. It also separates the being from its occupant — the voice holding the standing is not the one it started as, and the being is unmoved. Signed by the key the standing now commits to, and carrying no commitment of its own. Article XI: \"The commitment is present only when the message spends an heir, and is present whenever one is spent. A plain ask carrying one is refused, and a rotation carrying none is refused — Article XII's fourth step makes the carried commitment the new heir, and with nothing carried the step has no outcome and the standing could never change hands again.\" Which is Article VII's \"every rotation carries a fresh commitment, or a standing could be taken over once and never again\" read as a refusal rather than as advice. So: silence, and a standing that has not changed hands — the voice is still the one that rotated in above, and the mark has not been made fresh.",
